it more complicated than that  but it seemed that we had here a tissue  a chromosome where specific genes  specific nucleic acids it should be  possible to do something comparable to  the immunofluorescent staining technique  to detect and specific genes in these  tissues so actually I kept this in my  mind for quite a long time and it became  possible to think about doing this kind  of thing in the early 1960s when  investigators discovered that one could  take double-stranded DNA  denature it into a single strands and  immobilize those strands on a  nitrocellulose nitrocellulose filter  this was done by a couple of  investigators named Gillespie and  Spiegelman and what they showed was that  having the denatured DNA on a filter one  could then hybridize radioactive RNA to  it and actually determined and  quantitatively the amount of a  particular gene sequence on the filter  so I thought I I thought well if they  can do it on a filter you ought to be  able to do it on a slide so I tried  denaturing the DNA on a slide that is in  cells nuclei that were squashed out onto  a slide and I found that I could be  nature the DNA quite adequately  without damaging the tissue but then  when I tried to hybridize a radioactive  RNA to it I got no signal in this case I  was  looking for the radioactivity by  autoradiography that is by covering the  tissue with a thin layer of photographic  emulsion and looking for silver grains  in the emulsion caused by the  radioactivity of the probe as I said  nothing happened and I more or less gave  up on that a technique that was in the  early to mid-1960s  now a break came quite accidentally from  studies that I and others have been  carried out had been carrying out on  salamander oocytes  and frog oil sites and what we found was  that in the giant [ __ ] site nucleus  which is shown here derived from this  large oil site here the oocyte is the  egg of the frog inside of this giant  nucleus are of course the chromosomes  but in addition we found that there were  several hundred to a thousand nuclei  these round bodies here shown on this  particular slide now the interesting  thing about these nuclei that we  discovered was that they contained DNA  and it was specifically the genes that  code for ribosomal RNA that were in  these nuclei this is a most unusual  situation to have DNA outside of the  chromosomes but it was quite clear from  work that had been carried out in my  laboratory as well as in the laboratory  of Don Brown and of Oscar Miller that  there was extra chromosomal DNA outside  in these in the clear life and I studied  the origin of that DNA and what I found  was that it actually has was synthesized  at one specific time in the early  history of the formation of the oocyte  and one could see in these early cells  early nuclei that there was a cap  of DNA here and you can see the tap over  here you can see the chromosomes here  and down here you can see the  chromosomes in this nucleus so there's  in these two nuclei you have the  chromosome but then you have this huge  cap of extra DNA which in fact was the  ribosomal genes that had been somehow  and somehow come out of the chromosome  and amplified to give you this one  specific sequence a very large amount of  this one specific sequence so it  occurred to me that if there was any  possibility of doing in situ  hybridization this was the case that  would work and so I took a radioactive  ribosomal RNA and hybridized it to the  cells of this sort and I was quite  pleased to see the very first  experiments that we got good in situ  hybridization here this cell is black  because of the silver grains in the  photographic emulsion that have been  exposed by the radioactivity of the  hybridizing probe so this is a case  where we have hybridized radioactive RNA  ribosomal RNA to the ribosomal DNA in  the cell so that led immediately to a  publication with my student Mary Lou  Purdue who joined me with this in this  work and this was the 